d its limits. The paper argues that, although international law does not readily permit the closure of the Strait of Hormuz, the governing legal framework remains uncertain and contingent upon State acceptance and practice.", :title "Can Iran Restrict Navigation in the Strait of Hormuz? \"Between Treaty Law and Customary Uncertainty\"", :production_change false, :status "candidate", :uri "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6645778", :evidence_level "bibliographic-metadata", :peer_review "not-verified"} {:publication_type "book-chapter", :inspected_text "title-only", :proposed_test "Compare this candidate measurement with date-, geography-, unit-, coverage- and revision-matched canonical observations; disagreement that vanishes after matching falsifies the source-divergence explanation.", :source-sha256 "66ecef92c66eed14c794dea6ddb9f7851e37e371148d1f0d4d6033bbfb71d0c3", :indicators [], :abstract nil, :title "The Strait of Hormuz in Global Economics", :production_change false, :status "candidate", :uri "https://doi.org/10.1163/9789004635357_005", :evidence_level "bibliographic-metadata", :peer_review "not-verified"} {:publication_type "posted-content", :inspected_text "title-only", :proposed_test "Compare this candidate measurement with date-, geography-, unit-, coverage- and revision-matched canonical observations; disagreement that vanishes after matching falsifies the source-divergence explanation.", :source-sha256 "66ecef92c66eed14c794dea6ddb9f7851e37e371148d1f0d4d6033bbfb71d0c3", :indicators [], :abstract nil, :title "Strait of Hormuz crisis: how danger and uncertainty can affect the mental health of seafarers", :production_change false, :status "candidate", :uri "https://doi.org/10.64628/ab.jachkjtug", :evidence_level "bibliographic-metadata", :peer_review "not-verified"} {:publication_type "posted-content", :inspected_text "title-and-abstract", :proposed_test "Compare this candidate measurement with date-, geography-, unit-, coverage- and revision-matched canonical observations; disagreement that vanishes after matching falsifies the source-divergence explanation.", :source-sha256 "66ecef92c66eed14c794dea6ddb9f7851e37e371148d1f0d4d6033bbfb71d0c3", :indicators [], :abstract "Prolonged vessel lay-up can create an exceptional biosecurity hazard by allowing fouling assemblages to accumulate before vessels re-enter shipping networks. A recent study highlighted this risk following closure of the Strait of Hormuz and recommended pre-departure biofouling assessment, route forecasting, early detection and rapid communication. These measures address vectors and pathways, but species-targeted preparedness also requires a geographically resolved donor-region species baseline. Here, we use the recently validated Regional Organization for the Protection of the Marine Environment (ROPME) Sea Area (RSA) biosecurity baseline and integrate it with vessel-, route- and recipient-related information to support species–vessel–port prioritisation. The RSA baseline re-examined 136 species, of which 24 required reassignment or exclusion; 130 were retained and 62 added, yielding 192 species: 123 extant and 69 horizon. Thirty-nine extant species entered the very-high-priority tier, including seven benthic fouling organisms associated with shipping and ports. A watch list based on previous extant classifications would omit four of these seven. We therefore propose integrating validated donor-region occurrence with vessel exposure, route conditions, recipient susceptibility and response capacity. This framework provides an operational basis for species-targeted inspection, surveillance and rapid response and is transferable to future mass vessel lay-ups and other disruptions of maritime trade.", :title "Reopening the Strait of Hormuz: from a bioinvasion super-spreader warning to species–vessel–port prioritisation", :production_change false, :status "candidate", :uri "https://doi.org/10.32942/x2dh41", :evidence_level "bibliographic-metadata", :peer_review "not-verified"}], :limitation "Bounded literature search and available-abstract inspection. Full-text experimental claims and indicator adoption require review. Empty results do not establish absence.", :evidence [{:uri "https://api.crossref.org/works?query.bibliographic=Strait+of+Hormuz+visible+vessel+passages+measurement+reliability+coverage+uncertainty&rows=4&select=DOI%2Ctitle%2Ctype%2Cpublished%2Cabstract%2Clink", :raw-path "query-0.json", :sha256 "66ecef92c66eed14c794dea6ddb9f7851e37e371148d1f0d4d6033bbfb71d0c3", :retrieved-at "2026-11-07T17:46:56.504Z", :evidence-level :bibliographic-metadata, :http-status 200, :query "Strait of Hormuz visible vessel passages measurement reliability coverage uncertainty"} {:uri "https://api.crossref.org/works?query.bibliographic=Strait+of+Hormuz+visible+vessel+passages+alternative+indicators+usable+capacity+delivery+disruption&rows=4&select=DOI%2Ctitle%2Ctype%2Cpublished%2Cabstract%2Clink", :raw-path "query-1.json", :sha256 "e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855", :retrieved-at "2026-11-07T17:46:56.527Z", :evidence-level :bibliographic-metadata, :http-status 429, :query "Strait of Hormuz visible vessel passages alternative indicators usable capacity delivery disruption"}], :question "Does a newer independent observation confirm or contradict the last measured level?", :proposals [{:status :proposed, :indicator "Independent usable-capacity or recipient-delivery observations", :rationale "Physical passage counts alone do not establish deliverability.", :production-change? false}], :status :failed, :runtime {:node "v24.22.2"}, :max-cost-usd 0, :requests-attempted 2, :countermodels [{:status :proposed, :explanation "Coverage, sampling, or source revisions explain the apparent pattern."} {:status :proposed, :explanation "A sustained physical or economic constraint explains the pattern."}], :production-change? false}, :path "data/research/research-0b84843788fdfb6a3b5b95147b050b3c4a5152a30940d05568e68fb2bf3